    "note": "Amended 2023-04-13; the Assembly vote (2023-05-30) was on this text. Section 1 (Section 26165, in effect until Jan 1, 2026) STRIKES existing (b) (public live-fire standards with minimum rounds and scores), (c) (optional community college course up to 24 hours if required of all applicants), part of (d) (renewal course; trainers exempt from renewal training) and (e) (no payment for training before good-cause determination). Section 2 adds a new Section 26165 operative January 1, 2026, which is the DOJ curriculum, web-portal exam, instructor certification and recordkeeping text (only parts quoted; (d)-(f), (h), (j)-(n) omitted). Section 2's (a) and (b) repeat Section 1's."
  },
  "operative_text": "Section 26165 (Penal Code, Sec. 1, in effect until 2026): (b) For license renewal applicants, the course of training may be any course acceptable to the licensing authority, shall be no less than four hours, and shall satisfy the requirements of paragraphs (2) and (3) of subdivision (a).\nSection 26165 (Penal Code, Sec. 1, in effect until 2026): (c) This section shall remain in effect only until January 1, 2026, and as of that date is repealed.\nSection 26165 (Penal Code, Sec. 2, operative 2026): (c) The Department of Justice shall develop, evaluate, update, maintain, and publish standardized curricula for original-issue and renewal-issue instruction for a license to carry a pistol, revolver, or other firearm capable of being concealed upon the person, and shall develop, evaluate, update, maintain, and publish, where appropriate, test questions, written examinations, and testing formats for use in evaluating applicant class performance.\nSection 26165 (Penal Code, Sec. 2, operative 2026): (g) Any resident of this state making an application to carry a concealed firearm shall register on the state-maintained web portal, described in subdivision (e), and shall take a written examination offered and administered by the web portal.\nSection 26165 (Penal Code, Sec. 2, operative 2026): (i) A passing score of the written examination shall be no less than 80 percent correct answers of all questions on the examination.\nSection 26165 (Penal Code, Sec. 2, operative 2026): (o) Each issuing authority shall direct and refer all applicants only to those concealed carry instructors certified by the department meeting the ethical and professional standards as determined by the department, and who provide, at a minimum, the entire standardized curriculum and written examination, as determined by the department.\nSection 26165 (Penal Code, Sec. 2, operative 2026): (p) This section shall become operative on January 1, 2026."
}